Appendix L. Collusion Game Theory

L.1 Model

Let $u_i(\text{collude}, \text{not collude})$ be the payoff to authority $i$. Collusion is a Nash equilibrium if:

u_i(\text{collude}, \text{collude}) > u_i(\text{not collude}, \text{collude}) \quad \forall i.

L.2 Deterrence Mechanisms

Random audits. If detection probability is $p_d$ and penalty is $P$, collusion is deterred when:

p_d \cdot P > G

where $G$ is the gain from collusion.

Whistleblower rewards. Making defection profitable: $u_i(\text{defect}) > u_i(\text{collude})$.

Authority rotation. Limiting repeated interaction reduces the shadow of the future.

Cryptographic transparency. Making collusion evident in the audit log.

L.3 Bounds on Collusion Resistance

Proposition L.1. Under random audits with detection probability $p_d$, the maximum sustainable collusion gain is bounded by:

G_{\max} = \frac{p_d \cdot P}{1 - p_d}.

Proof. The expected payoff from collusion is $(1-p_d)G - p_d P$. For this to be non-positive: $G \leq p_d P / (1-p_d)$. $\square$

